Run ANGLE on HarmonyOS: its Vulkan and GLES displays, client extensions, GL strings and OH_NativeBuffer EGL images.

Absorbs, in this order: ohos-angle-vulkan-backend.patch, ohos-angle-gles-display.patch, ohos-angle-egl-client-extensions.patch, ohos-angle-null-gl-strings.patch, ohos-angle-native-buffer-image.patch. Their descriptions follow.

--- ohos-angle-vulkan-backend.patch

Teach ANGLE's Vulkan backend to present to a HarmonyOS window.

The Vulkan path was never going to work here and the reason was not the
device. ANGLE picks its window-system layer by platform, and OHOS defines
__linux__, so it was taken for desktop Linux -- which looks for X11,
Wayland, GBM or VK_KHR_display and finds none of them. That is where "the
device's Vulkan is missing VK_KHR_display" came from; a phone has no more
business with VK_KHR_display than Android does.

What the device has is the Android-shaped answer: VK_OHOS_surface,
vkCreateSurfaceOHOS taking an OHNativeWindow, VK_OHOS_external_memory and
VK_OHOS_native_buffer, and a libvulkan.so where ANGLE's loader already
looks. So this is the Android backend with three names changed, plus the
platform macro that stops OHOS being mistaken for Linux -- placed ahead of
the linux arm, since both match.

None of the Android-specific machinery comes over: the blobcache
properties are Android's, and AHardwareBuffer siblings have no counterpart
here. Recordable configs are left out for the same reason -- HarmonyOS
records through its own path.

This gets --use-angle=vulkan as far as creating a display and a surface.
Dawn, Graphite and a Vulkan-backed WebGPU all sit on top of this layer and
are not touched; they come after it works.

Three things only a compiler knew, all of them from copying the Android
backend without building it:

EGLNativeWindowType is an integer on this platform, not a pointer, so
comparing it with nullptr does not compile. vkCreateSurfaceOHOS is not in
the Vulkan headers ANGLE builds against -- those are Khronos's and have
never heard of this platform -- although the loader on the device has the
symbol, so the entry point is fetched at runtime the way ANGLE reaches
any extension it was not compiled with. And GenerateConfigs has no
overload taking sample counts.

Taking OHOS out of ANGLE_PLATFORM_LINUX is what stops the Vulkan backend
looking for X11, and it also takes OHOS out of every other ladder that
only knew Linux. The GL backend was the first: no include, so no GL at
all. It gets plain EGL, which is what Linux without X11 uses, and desktop
GL says there is none rather than building a display that cannot work.

--- ohos-angle-gles-display.patch

Give the GLES backend a display on HarmonyOS again.

The device renders nothing: the page area is white, SystemInfo reports
glImplementationParts (gl=none,angle=none) with no vendor, renderer or
version, and the log fills with

  ContextResult::kFatalFailure: Failed to create shared context for
  virtualization.
  SharedImageStub: unable to create context
  GpuChannel: Failed to create SharedImageStub

tens of thousands of times. None of that is the cause; all of it is what
happens downstream of ANGLE never producing a display.

ohos-angle-vulkan-backend.patch stopped OHOS from defining
ANGLE_PLATFORM_LINUX, which is what let the Vulkan backend stop looking
for X11. Display::CreateDisplayFromAttribs picks the backend from those
same macros, and its EGL_PLATFORM_ANGLE_TYPE_OPENGLES_ANGLE arm -- the
one --use-angle=gles-egl asks for, and the one the browser actually runs
-- knows Windows, Linux and Android:

    #    elif defined(ANGLE_PLATFORM_ANDROID)
                impl = new rx::DisplayAndroid(state);
    #    else
                // No GLES support on this platform, fail display creation.
                impl = nullptr;

OHOS used to reach the Linux arm and get a DisplayEGL, which is exactly
what it wants. It now reaches the else, and display creation fails. The
desktop GL arm above it had an #error, so the compiler demanded that one
be handled and it was; this one fails silently at runtime, so nothing
said anything until a device ran it.

DisplayEGL is the same implementation the Linux arm chose for
EGL_PLATFORM_ANGLE_DEVICE_TYPE_EGL_ANGLE, and there is no other device
type here, so this is unconditional as it is on Android.

--- ohos-angle-egl-client-extensions.patch

Advertise the EGL device type HarmonyOS asks ANGLE for.

build-4d5ad954 gave the GLES backend a display on OHOS and changed
nothing on a device: still glImplementationParts (gl=none,angle=none),
and on the phone not one GL or EGL line in the log.

That fix was right and never ran. Chromium asks for --use-angle=gles-egl
as EGL_PLATFORM_ANGLE_TYPE_OPENGLES_ANGLE together with
EGL_PLATFORM_ANGLE_DEVICE_TYPE_EGL_ANGLE (ui/gl/gl_display.cc), and
ANGLE validates that attribute before it creates anything:

    case EGL_PLATFORM_ANGLE_DEVICE_TYPE_EGL_ANGLE:
        if (!clientExtensions.platformANGLEDeviceTypeEGLANGLE)
        {
            val->setError(EGL_BAD_ATTRIBUTE, ...);
            return false;

and Display::GetClientExtensions() only sets that under
ANGLE_PLATFORM_LINUX -- which ohos-angle-vulkan-backend.patch stopped
OHOS defining. So eglGetPlatformDisplay returned EGL_NO_DISPLAY at
validation, CreateDisplayFromAttribs was never reached, and the failure
Chromium logs for it is a VLOG(1): silent in a release build. That is
the whole of what the phone showed.

Display.cpp has six places that choose by ANGLE_PLATFORM_LINUX. Four
choose a backend and were handled -- three because an #error made the
compiler insist, and the GLES one in ohos-angle-gles-display.patch. The
other two decide what the display advertises, fail silently, and are
this patch: the EGL device type above, and EGL_MESA_platform_surfaceless,
which OHOS also had while it was taken for Linux. Both are restored to
exactly what 52d6a0b7 had, the last build that rendered.

Nothing outside Display.cpp on this path chooses by that macro: libEGL,
libGLESv2 and the GL backend have no such branch.

--- ohos-angle-null-gl-strings.patch

Get GL strings through the HarmonyOS wrapper, and refuse a GL without them.

The GPU process in a native child of its own crashed in its first half
second, every time: SIGSEGV in strlen, from FunctionsGL::initialize via
DisplayEGL::createRenderer. glGetString(GL_VERSION) had returned null --
no current context, or a driver that would not answer in that process --
and it went straight into a std::string.

On OHOS a null version is now read as version 0.0 of GLES, which
DisplayEGL refuses, so EGL initialisation fails and Chromium's own
fallback takes over instead of the process dying. The extensions string
gets the same guard.

The first version checked for null and the process still crashed at the
same place, at address 0x1F02 -- GL_VERSION. glGetString had not
returned null; it had returned its argument, which is what the
HarmonyOS GLES wrapper's no-context stub does. So the guard is anything
below 64KiB, where nothing real lives.

And the cause, as far as it can be seen from outside: a probe in the same
process, reaching the system EGL and GLES through their exported symbols,
made a context current and read all three strings. ANGLE reaches them
through eglGetProcAddress, which can hand back entry points that bypass
the wrapper; a context made current that way does not install the
wrapper's dispatch table for the thread, and the wrapper's glGetString
then answers from the stub. In the browser process something else had
already made a context current through the wrapper, which is why it
worked there. FunctionsEGLDL now takes the wrapper's exports first --
only in a process that sets OHOS_ANGLE_WRAPPER_EXPORTS, which the GPU
process started as a native child of its own does, so the browser
process's working path is not touched.

--- ohos-angle-native-buffer-image.patch

Let ANGLE's GLES backend import HarmonyOS native buffers as EGLImages.

HarmonyOS shares GPU buffers as OH_NativeBuffer / OHNativeWindowBuffer, and
its EGL imports one as an EGLImage with target EGL_NATIVE_BUFFER_OHOS
(0x34E1, the client buffer being the OHNativeWindowBuffer) -- confirmed on a
Mate 70 Pro+ for 1080p P010 and NV12 buffers. It has no
EGL_EXT_image_dma_buf_import, and ANGLE's DisplayEGL imports nothing else,
so Chromium had no way to sample a native buffer: software-decoded video is
uploaded into textures every frame instead, which for 10-bit 1080p costs the
GPU thread 5-6 ms a frame.

This adds the passthrough, in the shape of DisplayEGL's dma-buf one:
EGL_NATIVE_BUFFER_OHOS is an external image target; DisplayEGL advertises
EGL_OHOS_image_native_buffer on OHOS (the system EGL lists no extension for
the target it accepts) and validates the target; and
NativeBufferImageSiblingOHOS tells the frontend the buffer's size and
format from its BufferHandle, with YUV formats (NV12, NV21, P010) marked
YUV for GL_TEXTURE_EXTERNAL_OES sampling. ImageEGL then creates the system
image with the same target and buffer. The sibling holds a reference on the
buffer for as long as the image exists.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.
Apply with:

  git -C /path/to/chromium/src apply /path/to/patches/ohos-angle.patch

